Q: LDAP search works via ldapsearch over VPN but fails in C# (System.DirectoryServices.Protocols) — connection/auth issue? A: <p>This is far too much code and the manual attempt at DNS resolution can only cause problems. A host name can map to multiple IPs, especially when VPNs are used. On top of that, using <code>Task.Run</code> to make a blocking method &quot;asynchronous&quot; can cause problems and results in overcomplicated code.</p>
<p>First, there's no need for a low-level LDAP connection. Since 2001 the high-level Directory search API is provided by the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.directoryservices?view=windowsdesktop-10.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer">System.DirectoryServices</a> namespaces, through the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.directoryservices.directorysearcher?view=windowsdesktop-10.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer">DirectorySearcher</a> and <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.directoryservices.directoryentry?view=windowsdesktop-10.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer">DirectoryEntry</a> class. At first, this was the only way to work with AD in PowerShell (which is built on top of .NET). There are hundreds of articles, blog posts and books on this. BUT after 25 years of documentation restructuring and migrations, even the Microsoft docs are kind-of-lost.</p>
<p>The <code>Using System.DirectoryServices</code> section of the docs be found <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/ms817874(v=msdn.10)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>, linking to quickstarts and task-specific examples. You'll note that the Table of Contents is broken, so you have to move around using links.</p>
<p>You can bind using a specific LDAP URL to a server or even a specific object, as the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/ms815618(v=msdn.10)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Binging Strings</a> docs show :</p>
<pre><code>var ent = new DirectoryEntry(&quot;LDAP://server01.mydomain.whatever&quot;,userName,password);
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<pre><code>var userEntry = new DirectoryEntry(&quot;LDAP://fabrikam.com/CN=Jeff Smith,OU=Marketing,DC=fabrikam,DC=Com&quot;,userName,password);
</code></pre>
<p>Once you have an entry, you can <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/ms817851(v=msdn.10)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">query its properties</a></p>
<pre><code>DirectoryEntry ent = new DirectoryEntry(&quot;LDAP://Fabrikam/CN=My Username,CN=Users,DC=Fabrikam,DC=com&quot;);
string name = ent.Properties[&quot;sn&quot;].Value.ToString();
{
     Console.WriteLine(name);
} 
</code></pre>
<p><code>DirectorySearcher</code> can be used to <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/ms817856(v=msdn.10)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">search or filter entries</a>. From the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/ms817856(v=msdn.10)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Setting Search Filters</a> example :</p>
<pre><code>DirectoryEntry entry = new DirectoryEntry(&quot;LDAP://CN=users,DC=fabrikam,DC=com&quot;);
DirectorySearcher mySearcher = new DirectorySearcher(entry);
mySearcher.Filter = &quot;(&amp;(objectClass=user)(anr=test*))&quot;;
SearchResultCollection ResEnt = mySearcher.FindAll();
{
        // Handle results.
}   
</code></pre>
